46.6.m:
"Burnt up is the demon," [he says],
for the striking away of the demons.
"O Agni, drive away the raw-flesh-eating fire," [he says]; having struck away that [fire] which eats raw flesh, he places it on the sacrificial ground fit for the sacrifice.
When he rolls a burning coal downwards,
he thereby places light in this world.
When he rolls it upwards,
he thereby places light in the atmosphere.
Yonder sun becomes light for him in that world.
He sets down three facing one another.
Three are the breaths: in-breath, through-breath, and out-breath;
these he places in the sacrificer.
Three are these worlds;
in these worlds he prospers.
"Firm you are; make firm the earth," [he says];
he makes firm the earth.
"Give life, give breath," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"A support you are; make firm the atmosphere," [he says];
he makes firm the atmosphere.
"Give sight, give hearing," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"A supporter you are; make firm the sky," [he says];
he makes firm the sky.
"Give vigour, give strength," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"An ordainment you are; make firm the quarters," [he says];
he makes firm the quarters.
"Give wealth, give abundance," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"A restraint you are; make firm the regions," [he says];
he makes firm the regions.
"Give form, give colour," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
Prajāpati, when he came into being in the beginning,
came into being by just so much.
One potsherd of the head comes into being in the beginning, then the second, then the third, then the fourth, then the fifth, then the sixth, then the seventh, then the eighth.
When he puts down eight facing one another,
the sacrificer thereby [prepares] himself […].

46.7.m:
[... When the sacrificial cake was set on [the fire]], the demons wished to slay it.
He is Agni, named Nāka in heaven, the slayer of demons.
He struck away the demons from it.
"May the divine Savitṛ cook you on the highest firmament," [he says],
for striking away the demons.
"May Agni not injure your body," [he says],
for non-injury.
He causes it to take on skin;
therefore the brain is covered around.
Those potsherds are the potsherds of the head;
that cake is the brain.
If he were not to cover it, the brain would be exposed;
because he covers it,
therefore the brain is in secret.
He covers it with ashes;
therefore the bone is covered with flesh.
He covers it over with the veda-broom;
therefore the head is covered with hairs.
The gods did not find [anyone] on whom [they thought], "We shall wipe off the cruel [remnant] of the sacrifice."
Agni said: "I shall produce for you someone on whom you will wipe off the cruel [remnant] of the sacrifice."
He dropped an ember into the waters.
From that Ekata was born;
a second [ember],
from that Dvita;
a third [ember],
from that Trita.
In that he fashioned them from himself, that is the nature of the Ātmeyas;
in that he fashioned them from the waters, that is the nature of the Āpeyas.
He pours it within the altar-ground;
he secures that very [essence].
He sprinkles it with a burning brand,
for cookedness;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
They went, wiping off [the guilt] onwards.
They wiped it off on the one upon whom the sun rises [while asleep];
the one upon whom the sun rises, on the one upon whom the sun sets [while asleep];
the one upon whom the sun sets, on the one with bad nails;
the one with bad nails, on the one with black teeth;
the one with black teeth, on the younger brother married before the elder;
the younger brother married before the elder, on the elder brother unmarried after the younger;
the elder brother unmarried after the younger, on the one who marries a younger sister before her elder sister;
the one who marries a younger sister before her elder sister, on the husband of a widow;
the husband of a widow, on the slayer of a warrior;
the slayer of a warrior, on the slayer of a Brahmin;
the slayer of a Brahmin, on the slayer of an embryo;
sin does not go beyond the slayer of an embryo.

46.8.m:
"At the impulse of the divine Savitṛ," [he says, and] takes up the wooden sword.
Impelled by Savitṛ he takes it up through the deities.
"You are Indra's right arm, thousand-spiked, hundred-edged," [he says].
The wooden sword is a thunderbolt;
he sharpens the thunderbolt
intending to strike at the rival.
"You are Vāyu, sharp-edged," [he says, and] wipes underneath.
"O earth, place of divine worship, may I not injure the root of your plant," [he says],
for the non-injury of the plants.
"Go to the enclosure, the cattle-pen," [he says].
The enclosure, the cattle-pen, is the metres;
he makes the metres an enclosure, a cattle-pen, for him.
"May heaven rain for you," [he says];
he pours down rain.
"Bind him, divine Savitṛ, on the furthest earth with a hundred bonds," [he says].
Two indeed are a man's rivals: he whom he hates, and he who hates him;
both of these he binds.
"May your droplet not leap to heaven," [he says].
The sap of this [earth] is the droplet;
on that these creatures live;
that he keeps in this [earth],
so that it will not flow away.
"May Araru not fly to heaven," [he says].
Araru is the rival;
he thrusts the rival away from the heavenly world.
There was an Asura named Araru;
he was afraid: "The gods will overcome us with the sacrifice."
He smeared the earth with poison, making it impure.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
his blood ran along this [earth];
[it became impure].
When he digs up,
whatever of it is impure, unfit for sacrifice, that he strikes away.
There was an Asura named Araru;
he was afraid: "The gods will drive me away from the earth."
He lay clinging to the earth.
Indra perceived him;
he struck him away from the earth, [saying,] "Drive away that Araru, unfit for divine worship, from the place of divine worship on earth."
He flew toward heaven.
With [the words,] "May Araru not fly to heaven," he thrust him back from heaven.
Thereupon the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
For whomsoever knowing thus the stambayajus is carried, [or] who knowing thus carries it, he removes the rival from these worlds;
he prospers himself, his rival is defeated.
[This earth belonged to the Asuras.]
[The gods said: "Gi]ve us [a share] of this."
They said: "Speak for yourselves."
Agni conquered the eastern quarter, the Vasus the southern, the Rudras the western, the Ādityas the northern.
The gods found this [earth] from the Asuras.
Thereupon the gods prospered, [the Asuras were defeated].
He who, knowing thus, encloses the altar with these deities, wins this [earth] of his rival; he prospers himself, his rival is defeated.
The pure and the impure departed from the earth;
the pure departed towards the east and north, the impure towards the west and south.
He should make the altar sloping toward the east and north;
he makes it pure, fit for sacrifice,
and for the separation of the pure and the impure.
He raises the two arms toward the east;
he encloses the Āhavanīya [fire].
The two buttocks are toward the west;
he encloses the Gārhapatya [fire].
The demons drink along the root;
he should not cut it with the fingernail.
If he were to cut it with the fingernail, he would become one with bad nails;
he cuts it with the wooden sword.
The wooden sword is a thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he strikes away the demons.

46.10.m:
"O divine waters, moving first," [he says];
he indeed brings forward the sacrifice.
"Lead forward this sacrifice, advance the master of the sacrifice," [he says];
he thereby invokes a blessing.
The essence of Agni departed;
having become a black antelope, he entered the trees.
"You are sprinkled," [he says]. "You are a black antelope dwelling in the pit; for Agni I sprinkle you, agreeable," [he says];
he thereby obtains that essence.
Moreover, for whichever deity he sprinkles it, for that [deity] he makes it agreeable.
"You are the altar; for the sacrificial grass I sprinkle you, agreeable." "You are the sacrificial grass; for the ladles I sprinkle you, agreeable," [he says].
The sacrificial grass is offspring, the altar is the earth;
he indeed establishes the offspring upon the earth.
"Go to the earth with sap," [he says];
he places sap in the earth.
Therefore offspring live upon the earth;
he becomes one upon whom [others] can live, who knows thus.
He should set down many [sprinkling waters];
as many sprinkling waters as he sets down, so many waters are his in yonder world;
therefore many are to be set down.
He takes the prastara-bunch from the east;
he makes the sacrificer the chief.
He should take as much as he can enclose with his hand;
the sacrificer becomes not hungry, the Adhvaryu does not become naked.
He should not shake it out;
if he were to shake it out, he would shake out his offspring and his cattle.
He should not wipe it;
whatever is his own, [whatever is his property], whatever is to be acquired, that he would wipe away.
He should not wipe it;
whatever is about to come to him, that he would thrust back.
He spreads the cord to the south;
therefore the pleat [of a garment] is on the south.
Holding the prastara-bunch, he encloses with the enclosing-sticks.
The prastara-bunch is the sacrificer;
the sacrificer himself thus encloses his own self
for the striking away of the demons.
He encloses with the enclosing-sticks joined together,
so that the demons may not enter.
He does not enclose in front;
yonder sun rising strikes away the demons from him in front.
He places two kindling-sticks upright;
he strikes away the demons from above.
He places two;
he makes a pair.
"You are the two arms of Savitṛ, the upholders of the divine folk," [he says];
for these two are Savitṛ's arms, the upholders of the divine folk.
"You are the seat of the Vasus, of the Rudras, of the Ādityas," [he says];
for whichever deities conquered, for them he makes this seat.
"Come, Juhū, anointed with ghee," [he says];
that which has ghee is their dear abode;
he provides them with their dear abode.
"Firm they have sat in the lap of cosmic order; protect them, O Viṣṇu," [he says].
Just as one would say, "This is your share, protect it," just so he says to Viṣṇu, "This is your clarified butter, protect it."

46.11.m:
"You are the world; spread out wide," [he says];
he indeed spreads [it] out.
"Agni is the sacrificer; this is homage," [he says];
for after having paid homage they wait upon a superior.
"Come, O ladle [Juhū], let Agni summon you to the divine worship," [he says];
Agni is all deities;
he obtains all deities.
"Come, O auxiliary ladle [Upabhṛt], let the divine Savitṛ summon you to the divine worship," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ he performs the rite [and] does not encounter misfortune.
"O two footprints of Viṣṇu, may I not step upon you with my foot," [he says];
[it is] as the sacrificial formula [states].
"Protect me, O Agni, from bad conduct; share me in good conduct," [he says];
he strikes off evil.
"Part asunder, do not scorch me," [he says],
for the non-injury of himself.
"Make a space for me, O two space-makers," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"From the place of Viṣṇu," [he says],
for firm establishment.
"The upright sacrificial rite touches the sky," [he says];
the sacrificial rite is indeed the sacrifice;
[it is] for the uprightness of the sacrifice.
"Unhurt is the sacrifice of the master of the sacrifice," [he says],
for prosperity.
"Possessing Indra, of great brilliance, svāhā!" [he says];
the sacrifice belongs to Indra;
he gives the sacrifice directly to Indra at the beginning;
his sacrifice becomes possessed of Indra.
"Together, light with light," [he says];
the sacrifice is light;
he connects sacrifice after sacrifice.
"By the impulse of strength, with Agni and Soma as the deity, I conquer," [he says];
whichever rival has previously surpassed him, him he thrusts away thereby.
"By the impulse of strength, with Agni and Soma as the deity, I repel that one," [he says];
whichever rival is behind him, him he pushes back thereby; he thrusts away a superior rival, he pushes back an inferior rival.
"For earth you, for the intermediate space you, for the sky you," [he says];
he makes it go to these very worlds.
"Licking [the ghee] anointed [on the grass], let the birds go," [he says];
he sweetens [it].
"Having become the dappled barren cow of the Maruts, variegated, go to the sky," [he says];
[the prastara-bunch is the sacrificer;]
[having become an oblation, he makes him go to the heavenly world;]
["From there stir up rain for us," he says;]
he obtains rain.
"Undiminished are the vital breaths," [he says];
thereby the Adhvaryu arranges the vital breaths at the head.
"You are the guardian of the eye, protect my eye," [he says],
for the protection of the eye.
"Which enclosing-stick you placed around, O divine Agni, being kindled by the Paṇis," [he says];
Agni is all deities;
he obtains all deities.
"Spread out the sacrificer," [he says];
he indeed spreads [him] out;
moreover, having made this sacrificer [an oblation], he makes him go to the heavenly world.
"Mighty one, having the remnants as your portion," [he says, and] offers over the enclosing-sticks;
he prospers them with their own rightful portion.
"O Agni, of uninjured lifespan, of unheated body," [he says];
what they omit, what cruel thing they do, that he appeases.
"Make our food free from poison," [he says];
he sweetens food for him.
"Well-placed wombs, the earth easy to sit on," [he says];
he settles him comfortably.
The altar departed from the gods;
they found it by means of the broom [veda];
that is the broomness of the broom.
That there is a broom [veda],
[is] for finding the altar [vedī].
The altar is female, the broom is male;
when he spreads the broom on the altar,
he obtains a pair.
